 here will invite the opponent to play to C8 and secure the edge.\n*   **Move (C, 8):** THIS is what I\'m looking for! Playing C8 gives me three pieces in a row on the right edge, flipping the opponent\'s D8 piece. This move removes an opponent\'s piece, increases stability, and secures edge control. The edge is a strategic area, and taking this position denies the opponent and secures an advantage. I must take this!\n*   **Move (E, 2):** This flips several pieces at once, but creates a straight line of my own pieces, or a "wall," which can be vulnerable in the future. I want to avoid giving the opponent this opportunity.\n*   **Move (G, 4):** Not immediately helpful. Gaining a piece with this inner position does not create an advantage.\n\nThe goal is to increase my own position\'s stability and block the opponent\'s possible plays. By playing C8, I am doing all of these. If I allow the opponent to C8, my position will be compromised. I have to secure this edge to control the game. Therefore, the choice is clear!\n\nI believe C8 is the winning move. This is the optimal strategic move.\n\nFinal Answer: (C, 8)\n\n\n', 'output': 'Situation: The opponent currently holds a significant material advantage (21 pieces to 3) and controls the center of the board. My pieces are clustered at E6, E7, and E8, giving me a foothold on the right-hand edge (Column 8). The opponent occupies D8, contesting this edge.\n\nEvaluation:\n- (C, 8) is the strongest move. It is an edge move that captures the opponent\'s piece at D8 by bracketing it with my piece at E8. This results in a solid block of three pieces (C8, D8, E8) on the edge, which is very stable and difficult for the opponent to attack.\n- (C, 6) and (C, 7) are inner moves that would flip D7 to my color. However, if D7 becomes mine, it provides the opponent with an anchor to play C8 themselves (capturing D7 and E6), which would be disastrous.\n- (A, 3) captures pieces but plays to an empty edge without the immediate support and stability that (C, 8) offers.\n- (E, 2) flips a vertical line of pieces, creating a large "frontier" (exposed pieces) that the opponent can easily exploit.\n\nReason: Move (C, 8) is the optimal choice because it secures the right-hand edge by establishing a connected group of stable pieces (C8-D8-E8). It removes the opponent\'s presence on that edge and prevents the opponent from taking that square later, which they could do if I played a move like C6 or C7.\n\nAnswer: (C, 8)'}
