nlikely to cause a problem for the current board state.\n\nBased on this analysis, I'm going with (G, 4). This move is strategic because it minimizes the flip count (which helps keep my future move options open) and creates a safe expansion without giving the opponent an immediate counterattack. Also, G4 consolidates my position a bit. I will play this move.\n\nOutput:\n**(G, 4)**\n\n\n", 'output': "Situation: The game is in the mid-game phase. I control a cluster of pieces in the bottom-right corner, while the opponent has a strong central influence. The left side of the board is open. I have four valid options: one on the top edge (A, 3), two expanding to the left (D, 2) and (E, 2), and one internal move (G, 4).\n\nEvaluation: \n- Move (A, 3) captures three pieces along a diagonal but places a piece on the edge adjacent to an opponent's piece. This would likely allow the opponent to respond at A4, flipping my piece back and solidifying a strong position on the top edge (A2-A5).\n- Moves (D, 2) and (E, 2) both flip four pieces and create a horizontal wall of my color on rows D and E respectively. Creating walls is generally risky in Othello as it provides the opponent with many anchor points to flank my pieces later, reducing my mobility.\n- Move (G, 4) is a quiet internal move that flips only two pieces (F5, E6). It connects my existing group without creating a large frontier or giving up edge stability.\n\nReason: (G, 4) is the strategically superior choice. It follows the principle of minimizing flipped pieces (evaporation strategy) to limit the opponent's future mobility. It avoids the risks of building vulnerable walls (like D2/E2) or allowing the opponent to stabilize the edge (like A3). It safely consolidates my territory in the lower-center.\n\nAnswer: (G, 4)"}
